QUICK BITE
A food ordering web application with separate customer and admin interfaces, built with Java servlets, JSP, and MySQL.
Features
For customers
- Register, log in, log out
- Browse the menu and view product details
- Place orders
- View order history
- Update profile
For admins
- Dashboard showing today's revenue, completed orders, and average order value
- Add, edit, and delete products
- Manage product images
- Update order status
- View sales reports
Technologies
Area
What I used
Language
Java
Web framework
Jakarta Servlet API
View layer
JSP, CSS
Database
MySQL
Data access
JDBC
Security
BCrypt password hashing, session-based access control
Helpers
Gson for JSON
Project structure
src/main/java/com/quick_bite/
├── controller/       Servlets for the customer side
├── servlet/          Servlets and filters for the admin side
├── dao/              Database access — all SQL lives here
├── model/            Domain classes
└── sqlconnection/    Database connection setup

src/main/webapp/
├── admin/            Admin pages
├── customer/         Customer pages
└── WEB-INF/          web.xml and library JARs
How the code is organised
The project follows a layered structure where each layer has one job:
Servlet layer — handles HTTP requests and responses. Servlets do not write SQL.
DAO layer — owns the database queries. Each DAO class maps to a specific kind of record, for example OrderDAO or ProductDAO.
Model layer — holds the data classes. These have no knowledge of the database.
So the flow is: a servlet receives a request → calls a DAO → the DAO runs the query and returns model objects → the servlet passes them to a JSP page.
Things I focused on
Protecting the admin area in one place. Instead of adding a login check inside every admin page, NoCacheFilter checks the whole /admin/ area at once. Pages that should always be reachable, like the login page and CSS files, are listed as exceptions. Anything else requires a logged-in admin session, otherwise the user is redirected.
Writing queries safely. Every SQL statement uses PreparedStatement with parameters. User input is never inserted into a query string.
Closing resources properly. Database connections, statements, and results are closed using try-with-resources, so they are always released even if something fails.
Avoiding repeated queries. OrderDAO.getAllOrders() needs each order together with its items and product names. Instead of running a new query for every single order, it runs one query using table joins and builds up the order objects as the results come back.
Running the project
You will need:
- JDK 17 or newer
- MySQL 8 or newer
- Tomcat 10.1 or newer
There is no build file for this project. It is opened in IntelliJ IDEA and run on Tomcat.
Step 1 — Create the database
Create a MySQL database named quick_bite, then write a SQL script to create the tables:
orders, order_items, products, product_images, payments, customers, admins, activity
Check the SQL queries inside dao/OrderDAO.java and dao/PaymentDAO.java to match the column names used there.
Step 2 — Set your database password
Open sqlconnection/DBConnection.java and put in your own MySQL username and password.
Do not commit a real password to a public repository. If you plan to push this project to GitHub, read the note near the bottom of this page first.
Step 3 — Add the library files
These go in src/main/webapp/WEB-INF/lib/. They are not included in the repository, so you download them yourself:
File
What it is for
mysql-connector-j-9.4.0.jar
Connects Java to MySQL
jbcrypt-0.4.jar
Securely hashes passwords
gson-2.10.1.jar
Handles JSON
Step 4 — Run it
1. Open the project in IntelliJ IDEA.
2. Add a Tomcat 10.1+ server.
3. Set the project artifact to war:exploded.
4. Start the server.
5. Go to http://localhost:8080/quick_bite/
A note on the database password
DBConnection.java originally contained a real MySQL password in plain text. That file was public, so anyone could read it.
If you use this project, put your credentials in environment variables instead of writing them in the code:
String url = System.getenv("DB_URL");
String user = System.getenv("DB_USER");
String password = System.getenv("DB_PASSWORD");
Then set those three values on your machine before running the project. Never push real passwords to GitHub — and remember that deleting a file does not remove it from the repository history.
Notes
- The library files, the build folder, and IntelliJ settings are excluded using .gitignore, so they must be added locally.
- Classes named ProductImage, TopProduct, and WeeklySalesReport support the reporting and product features.
- The servlets use the newer jakarta.servlet package, but web.xml still declares version 2.4. The project runs on Tomcat 10.1+, and updating web.xml to version 5.0 would make it consistent.
